x-alpha is at 90 percent disk with 45G free and six per-lane target dirs holding 82G: the B106 ENOSPC false-green route is one concurrent lane away from reopening

MEASURED on x-alpha, 2026-09-01, by lane-tooling while costing B163's design:

  458G total, 390G used, 45G free -- 90% full.
  Six per-lane target dirs: 9.6 / 11 / 14 / 15 / 16 / 16 GB = ~82 GB.
  B106's floor: 10 GiB.

WHY THIS IS URGENT RATHER THAN HOUSEKEEPING. B106's incident was x-alpha at 0
bytes with GATES EXITING 0 WHILE BUILDS FAILED WITH ENOSPC -- a false green on
the instrument every lane's evidence depends on. The floor guard exists because
of it. We are currently 35 GB above that floor while running four concurrent
lanes at ~14 GB of target dir each. One additional concurrent tree, or one lane
doing a clean rebuild, closes the gap.

This is also what killed B163's live-merge-base-build option: it would have
needed a SECOND target dir per lane (reusing the lane's own thrashes the
incremental cache between two source trees, which is why remote-build.py isolates
them at all), i.e. ~56 GB against 45 GB free. Buying a set-diff by reopening a
false-green route in every other gate on the box.

WHAT TO DO -- and CAUTION, this is a SHARED build box, not ours alone:
  1. Enumerate the six target dirs and map each to a worktree that still exists.
     `remote-build.py` names the remote tree per worktree, so an orphan is a
     target dir whose lane worktree is gone. DO NOT infer staleness from mtime
     alone -- a lane can idle for hours mid-ticket and this batch has had lanes
     hold for 40 minutes at a stretch.
  2. Confirm with the coordinator which lanes are live BEFORE deleting anything.
     There are currently four active lanes plus recently-removed worktrees, so
     the orphan set is real but is not simply "the oldest two".
  3. Only then reclaim. `cargo clean` in an orphan tree, or remove the orphan
     remote tree entirely.

STRUCTURAL FOLLOW-UP, worth more than the one-off cleanup: nothing currently
removes a remote tree or target dir when a lane worktree is cleaned up locally.
Every completed lane in this repo's history has left ~14 GB on the build box
forever. The reclaim should be part of worktree teardown, not a periodic manual
sweep -- otherwise this ticket recurs on a schedule set by how fast we close
tickets.

Also worth checking whether B106's floor check runs BEFORE the build or only
guards the reported result. A floor that is consulted after a build has already
consumed the disk protects the report but not the box.

Related: B164 (a stale baseline binary the sweep guard cannot see), B106 (the
original ENOSPC false green and the 10 GiB floor).

Decision 2026-09-01: serial tooling lane after B164 because both change scripts/remote-build.py and remote-tree lifecycle. First enumerate remote trees and map them to extant local worktrees; no shared-host deletion occurs until the coordinator confirms the orphan set. Then add a durable teardown/reclaim path and prove the disk-floor preflight runs before build/sync.